=== WHAT EACH PUBLISHED CONTRAST IMPLIES, GIVEN THE MEASURED S ===
  A DW-minus-NDW contrast is bounded by BETWEEN-EVENT variation, not by S,
  so 'contrast vs S' is the wrong comparison and an earlier draft of this
  script made it. The right question: with a mean causal effect S and a DW
  fraction q, a contrast C forces the two subgroups to be
      DW  =  S + (1-q)*C        NDW  =  S - q*C
  and it is the implied NDW value that is testable.

reported quantity                                     C     q      DW     NDW
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Karpechko-criterion AO contrast, observations     1.782  0.70  +1.892  +0.110
CMIP6 ensemble DW-NDW contrast                    1.143  0.50  +1.929  +0.786
ACP 26, 3723 (2026) published NAO contrast        0.850  0.59  +1.706  +0.856
ERA5 1000 hPa NAM contrast, Karpechko criterion   0.684  0.54  +1.672  +0.988
ERA5 850 hPa NAM contrast, ACP criterion          0.639  0.59  +1.620  +0.981

  READ HONESTLY, S = +1.358 sigma:
   - 1 of 5 contrasts force NDW to zero or REVERSED sign:
       Karpechko-criterion AO contrast, observations  ->  NDW = +0.110
   - the other 4 imply NDW responses of +0.79 to +0.99 sigma, i.e. events labelled
     'non-propagating' would STILL carry a large real surface response.
     That is a CONTINUUM, not two populations, and it is exactly what a
     threshold cut through one shifted distribution looks like.

  So SNAPSI does NOT show the published contrasts exceed what the
  stratosphere can cause -- an earlier draft of this script claimed that and
  it was wrong. What it shows is that a large mean effect (S = +1.36
  sigma, measured causally) plus a threshold split reproduces most of those
  contrasts WITHOUT any second population; only the most extreme reported
  contrast demands one, and it demands a physically odd one.
